From 750dfe3822b40985a138481dd573331119f5535c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bb-machine <ubuntu@bb-machine>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:09:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(p6r): skip participant projection rebuilds for unchanged
 threads

executeThreadFacetQuery rebuilt the native participant projection for
every thread on every result page. That meant reading and zod-parsing
every qualifying client/turn/requested event of all 100 threads, then
delete-and-reinserting their profile rows, even though every write
that changes those inputs already reprojects inside its own
transaction. On bb-machine the first page of active threads took
~1.8 s, and a pass over all active threads ~3.2 s of reads, almost all
for unchanged projections.

Each projection now records a watermark in a new p6r sidecar table,
p6r_participant_watermarks. The watermark is the latest request
event's sequence and id, the contribution count and latest acceptance
time, and a projector version. It is written in the same transaction
as every replacement: the event-append, contribution-acceptance and
queued admission/dispatch projections, and the read path. The read
path computes current watermarks with one indexed query per page and
rebuilds only threads whose watermark is missing or differs. The
output of a rebuild is unchanged. The migration only creates the
table; watermarks fill on first rebuild.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
